<p>Open area varies widely by construction. For example, Intralox lists its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-900/perforated-flat-top" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 900 Perforated Flat Top</a> modular plastic belt at 5-8% open area depending on hole size, with temperature ranges of 34 to 220 F in polypropylene and 34 to 200 F in acetal. Get open area and temperature ratings from the belt manufacturer for the exact construction you are quoting.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Cooling Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Bakery</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">Bakery cooling</a> is the largest application for cooling conveyors. Bread must cool from oven-exit temperature until it can be sliced and bagged without condensation. Cookies must cool before wrapping to keep their texture. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">Cookie and cracker lines</a> need gentle handling during cooling to prevent breakage.</p>
<h3>Confectionery</h3>
<p>Chocolate-enrobed products (bars, nuts, pretzels) pass through a cooling tunnel to set the chocolate coating. The tunnel controls air temperature and humidity to prevent bloom (white discoloration from fat or sugar crystal migration). Belt speed and tunnel length set the cooling time the coating needs. Cooling tunnels for confectionery use stainless steel mesh belts and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary frame construction</a>.</p>
<h3>Meat and Poultry</h3>
<p>Cooked meat and poultry products must be cooled within the time and temperature limits in FSIS stabilization guidance to limit pathogen growth. Spiral coolers with refrigerated air handling achieve rapid cooling in a compact footprint. Stainless steel construction and IP69K-rated components (tested with 80 C water at 80-100 bar, per <a href="https://keystonecompliance.com/ip69k-ingress-protection-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Keystone Compliance</a>) handle the high-pressure washdown common in meat plants.</p>
<h3>Industrial and Non-Food</h3>
<p>Cooling conveyors serve non-food applications including plastic part cooling after injection molding, metal part cooling after heat treatment, rubber product cooling after vulcanization, and glass cooling after forming. These applications use carbon steel mesh belts (no food-grade requirement) and ambient or forced-air cooling. Part weight and temperature determine belt construction and support structure.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Sizing a Cooling Conveyor</h2>
<p>Cooling conveyor sizing starts with required cooling time, the time needed to reduce product from inlet temperature to target temperature. Cooling time depends on product thermal properties (mass, specific heat, thermal conductivity, surface area), cooling medium temperature, and air velocity.</p>
<p>A simplified sizing approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Determine required cooling time</strong> from product testing or heat transfer calculations. For bakery products, empirical data from similar products is the most reliable source.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate belt length</strong> = belt speed (FPM) x cooling time (minutes). For spirals, this is the total belt path length inside the tower.</li>
<li><strong>Determine belt width</strong> from product width, number of lanes, and edge clearance. Confirm the edge clearance the supplier needs for side guides and tracking.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate throughput</strong> = products per belt foot x belt speed (FPM). Verify that throughput meets production requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Add a design margin</strong> for product variation, ambient temperature changes, and belt loading inefficiency. Agree on the margin with the supplier and base it on trial data where possible.</li>
</ol>
<p>Cost rises from straight-line ambient conveyors (belt and structure only), to forced-air tunnels (adding the enclosure, air handling, and controls), to spiral coolers (adding the tower drive and structure, but delivering the most cooling capacity per square foot of floor space). Refrigerated and cryogenic systems add refrigeration plant or gas supply costs. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed cost factors.</p>
