<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a cooling conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A cooling conveyor transports hot or warm products through a cooling zone (ambient air, forced air, or refrigerated) to reduce temperature before packaging. Uses mesh belts for airflow. Common in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">bakery</a>, confectionery, meat, and industrial applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Spiral cooler vs. straight-line cooler?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Spirals stack belt tiers vertically, providing long cooling time in a small footprint. Straight-line coolers run horizontally. They are simpler and cheaper but use far more floor space for the same cooling time. Spirals suit medium and high-volume operations; straight-line suits small operations with available floor space.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do you calculate cooling conveyor length?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt length = belt speed (FPM) x required cooling time (minutes). Required cooling time depends on product thermal properties, cooling method, and air velocity. Forced air shortens cooling time compared with ambient. Add a design margin based on trial data.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What belt types are used for cooling?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Wire mesh belts (stainless or carbon steel, high open area), modular plastic (perforated, lower open area), and flat wire compound belts. Mesh size must support the product while maximizing airflow. FDA-compliant materials required for food contact.</p>

</div>
